# Security headers for every route, in the Netlify/Cloudflare Pages format.
# Gatsby copies this file from static/ into the publish directory, and
# gatsby-node.js then rewrites the CSP placeholder below. Any host that does
# not read a _headers file must replicate the *rewritten* headers from
# public/_headers in its own configuration; see docs/RELEASE.md.
#
# Notes on the CSP.
#
# script-src carries no 'unsafe-inline'. Gatsby emits a small, fixed set of
# inline bootstrap scripts (the page path, the chunk mapping, the compilation
# hash), so they are allowlisted by SHA-256 instead. Those hashes change on
# every build, so the placeholder token in script-src below is substituted at
# build time rather than written out here; onPostBuild fails the build if that
# token is missing, so this cannot silently regress to a policy that allows
# arbitrary inline script. A browser ignores 'unsafe-inline' whenever a hash
# or nonce is present anyway, so the two cannot be combined as a fallback.
#
# style-src keeps 'unsafe-inline': styled-components injects rules through the
# CSSOM at runtime, which no hash can cover. That is a far weaker exposure
# than inline script and is accepted.
#
# connect-src names the three Horizon hosts and nothing else. The activity
# table is the only part of the page that reads the network directly, and it
# reads it from the URL the snap reports (`getNetworkDetails().networkUrl`),
# which can only ever be one of these three. Listing them explicitly means a
# future change that starts talking to some other endpoint fails visibly in
# the browser rather than quietly succeeding. Everything the snap itself does
# happens in the snap's own sandbox and is not governed by this policy.
#
# Fonts are self-hosted under /fonts, so they need no origin of their own and
# 'self' covers them. That is a large part of why they are self-hosted: a font
# CDN would mean adding a remote origin here and announcing every visitor of a
# wallet page to a third party.
#
# Everything else is locked to 'self'. frame-ancestors 'none' (with the
# X-Frame-Options fallback) prevents the dapp from being framed for
# clickjacking a user's snap interactions.
#
# Permissions-Policy turns off every powerful browser feature the page has no
# use for (camera, microphone, geolocation, payment, USB, serial, and the
# rest), for this origin and for any frame it might ever embed. A wallet page
# needs none of them, and a policy that says so means an injected script, or
# a future dependency, cannot quietly ask the user for one under this
# origin's name. MetaMask's own UI lives in the extension, outside this
# document, and is not affected.
#
# Cross-Origin-Opener-Policy: same-origin severs the window reference between
# this page and any page that opened it (or that it opens), so a site that
# popped the dapp open cannot reach back into it through window.opener, and
# the dapp's own window.open targets cannot reach back into the dapp. It
# also puts the page in its own browsing context group, which is the
# precondition for cross-origin isolation should it ever be wanted.
/*
  Content-Security-Policy: default-src 'self'; script-src 'self' {{INLINE_SCRIPT_HASHES}}; style-src 'self' 'unsafe-inline'; img-src 'self' data:; font-src 'self'; connect-src 'self' https://horizon.stellar.org https://horizon-testnet.stellar.org https://horizon-futurenet.stellar.org; object-src 'none'; frame-ancestors 'none'; base-uri 'self'; form-action 'self'
  X-Frame-Options: DENY
  Referrer-Policy: no-referrer
  X-Content-Type-Options: nosniff
  Strict-Transport-Security: max-age=31536000; includeSubDomains
  Permissions-Policy: accelerometer=(), autoplay=(), camera=(), display-capture=(), encrypted-media=(), fullscreen=(self), geolocation=(), gyroscope=(), magnetometer=(), microphone=(), midi=(), payment=(), picture-in-picture=(), publickey-credentials-get=(), screen-wake-lock=(), serial=(), usb=(), xr-spatial-tracking=()
  Cross-Origin-Opener-Policy: same-origin
